      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Definition"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Zero conditional expresses general truths and universal facts using Simple Present in both clauses.",
        "First conditional refers to real or likely future situations using Simple Present in the conditional clause and Simple Future in the main clause.",
        "Second conditional expresses imaginary, hypothetical, or improbable present/future situations using Simple Past in the 'if' clause and 'would + V1' in the main clause.",
        "Third conditional represents impossible past circumstances using Past Perfect ('had + V3') in the 'if' clause and 'would have + V3' in the main clause.",
        "Conditional words such as 'unless', 'provided that', 'as long as', and 'supposing' can replace 'if' depending on context."
      ],
      "id": "cd8ifz6"
    },
